<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Fixes</a> / External SSD not showing up</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Storage troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">External SSD not showing up in Windows 11.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">An external SSD that doesn’t appear in File Explorer usually falls into one of four cases: Windows can’t see it at all, it’s brand new and has no partition yet, it has no drive letter, or it was formatted on a Mac. Disk Management tells you which one you have in under a minute.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>Quick answer</strong><p>Plug the SSD into a USB port directly on the computer, using the cable that came with it. Then right-click Start and open <strong>Disk Management</strong>. If a <strong>brand-new</strong> SSD shows as <strong>Not Initialized</strong>, initialize it and create a volume. If it shows a volume with no letter, assign one. If it was used on a Mac, it’s probably APFS, which Windows can’t read. If it doesn’t appear in Disk Management at all, the cable, port, or enclosure is the problem. <strong>Never initialize or format a drive that has files you need.</strong></p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Symptoms</p><h2>What you might see.</h2>
<ul class="article-list">
<li>The SSD’s light comes on, but nothing appears in File Explorer.</li>
<li>Windows plays the device-connected sound, but there’s no new drive.</li>
<li>Disk Management shows a disk labelled <strong>Unknown</strong> and <strong>Not Initialized</strong>, or a block of <strong>Unallocated</strong> space.</li>
<li>Windows says “You need to format the disk in drive X: before you can use it.” If the drive has your files, go to <a href="/guides/external-drive-says-you-need-to-format-it/">the format prompt guide</a> instead.</li>
<li>Nothing happens at all when you plug it in.</li>
</ul></section>

