<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Connect it directly, with the right cable.</h2>
<p><strong>Do this:</strong> Unplug any hub or dock. Plug the SSD into a USB port on the computer itself, using the cable that came with the SSD. On a desktop, use a rear port on the motherboard rather than a front-panel port.</p>
<p><strong>Why:</strong> Some USB-C cables are made for charging and carry little or no data, so the SSD can light up without Windows ever seeing it. Hubs and front-panel ports add connections that can drop out under load.</p>
<p><strong>Expect:</strong> The connect sound, and the drive appearing in File Explorer or Disk Management. If nothing happens, try another port, then another computer. If it fails everywhere with a known-good cable, the SSD or its enclosure is the likely fault.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Open Disk Management and find the SSD.</h2>
<p><strong>Do this:</strong> Right-click the Start button and choose <strong>Disk Management</strong>. Find the disk whose size matches your SSD. A 1 TB SSD shows as about 931 GB, because Windows counts storage differently from the box.</p>
<p><strong>Why:</strong> Disk Management shows drives that File Explorer hides, and tells you which of the cases below you have.</p>
<p><strong>Expect:</strong> One of these states, covered in the next steps:</p>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Not listed</strong><p>Windows doesn’t see the drive. Go back to Step 01, then see Step 06.</p></div>
<div><strong>Not Initialized or Unallocated</strong><p>See Step 03, and read its warning first.</p></div>
<div><strong>A volume with no drive letter</strong><p>See Step 04.</p></div>
<div><strong>A volume Windows can’t read</strong><p>See Step 05. Mac-formatted drives often show this way.</p></div>
</div>
<p>You can also check from PowerShell, which only reads information: <code>Get-Disk</code> lists every disk, with its <code>PartitionStyle</code> showing <code>RAW</code> for a disk that has no partition table.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Brand-new SSD: initialize it and create a volume.</h2>
<div class="note note-warn"><strong>Warning:</strong> Initializing erases everything on the disk. Do this only on a brand-new SSD, or one whose contents you don’t need. If a drive that held your files suddenly shows <strong>Not Initialized</strong>, stop. Don’t initialize it. Recover the files first, starting with <a href="/guides/external-drive-says-you-need-to-format-it/">this guide</a>.</div>
<p><strong>Do this:</strong></p>
<ol class="article-list">
<li>If the disk says <strong>Offline</strong>, right-click it and choose <strong>Online</strong>.</li>
<li>Right-click the disk label on the left (for example, “Disk 2”) and choose <strong>Initialize Disk</strong>. Leave the default, <strong>GPT</strong>, and click <strong>OK</strong>.</li>
<li>Right-click the <strong>Unallocated</strong> space and choose <strong>New Simple Volume</strong>.</li>
<li>Accept the full size, pick a drive letter, and choose a file system. Use <strong>NTFS</strong> for Windows only, or <strong>exFAT</strong> if you also need it on a Mac.</li>
<li>Click <strong>Finish</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Why:</strong> Microsoft notes that a new disk isn’t available in File Explorer until it has been initialized and given a volume. Some external SSDs come ready to use, and others don’t.</p>
<p><strong>Expect:</strong> The drive appears in File Explorer with the letter you chose.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Volume with no drive letter: assign one.</h2>
<p><strong>Do this:</strong> Right-click the volume (the bar on the right, not the disk label), choose <strong>Change Drive Letter and Paths</strong>, click <strong>Add</strong> or <strong>Change</strong>, pick an unused letter, and click <strong>OK</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Why:</strong> Without a letter, Windows has no way to show the drive in File Explorer. This sometimes happens when another device has taken the letter the SSD used before.</p>
<p><strong>Expect:</strong> The drive appears in File Explorer immediately, with your files intact. Assigning a letter doesn’t change the data.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Used on a Mac: Windows can’t read the format.</h2>
<p><strong>Do this:</strong> If the SSD was set up on a Mac, plug it back into the Mac and check its format in Disk Utility. If it says APFS or Mac OS Extended, copy the files off on the Mac. Then erase it as <strong>exFAT</strong> to use it on both.</p>
<p><strong>Why:</strong> Apple lists exFAT and MS-DOS (FAT) as the formats that work with Windows, with exFAT for volumes over 32 GB. Windows doesn’t read APFS without extra software.</p>
<p><strong>Expect:</strong> After reformatting as exFAT, the drive opens on both the Mac and Windows. Don’t let Windows format a Mac drive that still has your only copy of the files.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 06</p><h2>Not listed anywhere: check Device Manager and the enclosure.</h2>
<p><strong>Do this:</strong> Right-click Start, open <strong>Device Manager</strong>, and expand <strong>Disk drives</strong>. Plug and unplug the SSD and watch whether an entry appears. If it appears with a warning icon, right-click it, choose <strong>Uninstall device</strong>, unplug the SSD, restart, and plug it back in.</p>
<p><strong>Why:</strong> If Device Manager sees the SSD but Disk Management doesn’t, the problem is the driver or Windows. If neither sees it, the problem is physical: the cable, port, enclosure, or drive.</p>
<p><strong>Expect:</strong> The drive reappears after the restart. If it still doesn’t appear on any computer with a known-good cable, the drive or its enclosure has likely failed. If it’s an NVMe drive in a separate enclosure, test the drive in another enclosure or directly in a PC before deciding which part is faulty.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Verification</p><h2>Confirm it’s fixed.</h2>
<ul class="article-list">
<li>The SSD appears in File Explorer with a drive letter and the expected capacity.</li>
<li>Copy a large file (a few GB) to it and back, then open the copy.</li>
<li>Unplug it using <strong>Eject</strong>, plug it back in, and make sure it returns with the same letter.</li>
</ul>
<p>If it disconnects during the large copy, go back to the cable and port checks. Repeated drops under load often point to the cable, a hub, or the enclosure.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Should you buy anything?</p><h2>When a purchase actually fixes it.</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Don’t buy anything yet</strong><p>If the SSD isn’t initialized, has no letter, or uses a Mac format. Those are free settings fixes.</p></div>
<div><strong>A data cable</strong><p>If it works with one cable and not another. A replacement rated for USB data at the SSD’s speed is the cheapest fix.</p></div>
<div><strong>A new enclosure</strong><p>If the NVMe drive works when tested elsewhere but not in its enclosure. Match the enclosure to the drive type (NVMe or SATA) and size.</p></div>
<div><strong>A new SSD</strong><p>If the drive isn’t detected anywhere, or keeps failing. If it held files you need, try recovery or a professional service before replacing it.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">How this guide was made</p><p>These steps are based on Microsoft and Apple documentation, linked below. StackGeist has not tested a specific SSD or enclosure for this guide.</p></section>

